cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use corona3d in your work, please cite it, together with the model run, the coronagraph data, and the methods that it lists for each layer."
title: "corona3d: a three-dimensional reconstruction of a coronal mass ejection and the corona from public MHD, coronagraph, and radio data"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Aggarwal
    given-names: Keshav
    affiliation: "Space Physics Laboratory (SPL), Vikram Sarabhai Space Centre (VSSC), ISRO"
    email: keshavaggarwal2310@gmail.com
version: 0.1.0
license: MIT
date-released: 2026-10-27
abstract: >-
  corona3d is a physics-based Python package that reconstructs the solar corona, a coronal mass
  ejection (CME), and the sources of its radio burst in three dimensions for any event from public
  data, following the method described in Morosan et al. (2022). It renders the electron-density
  surface of a PSI MAS/MAST magnetohydrodynamic model at a chosen plasma level coloured by the
  Alfven speed, together with open and closed coronal field lines and the closed lines through the
  CME shell, using PyVista and VTK. The graduated cylindrical shell of the CME is fitted to
  coronagraph images with PyThea, and the white-light images of SOHO/LASCO C2 and of the STEREO
  COR1 and COR2 coronagraphs are drawn as ratio or difference planes in the plane of the sky of
  their spacecraft. The sources of a radio burst are measured in images of the Nancay
  Radioheliograph and of the Owens Valley Radio Observatory Long Wavelength Array as coloured blobs
  and contours, which are placed at their Newkirk heights on the side of the plane of the sky that
  faces the observer. Each panel is drawn with an orthographic projection from Earth, STEREO-A or
  STEREO-B, the solar north pole, or any chosen direction, and every layer can be switched on or
  off. A command-line interface driven by one configuration file downloads the data, fits the
  shell, chooses the radio interval, and draws and exports the reconstruction. The CME of 2013
  October 25, studied by Morosan et al. (2022), is the default configuration and the benchmark
  against which the method is validated, and the CME and type II burst of 2024 May 9 is a new
  application.
repository-code: "https://github.com/jovian-explorer/corona3d"
url: "https://jovian-explorer.github.io/software.html#corona3d"
keywords:
  - solar corona
  - coronal mass ejections
  - MHD
  - Alfven speed
  - type II radio bursts
  - LASCO
  - STEREO
  - GCS
